A NOTE ON WHAT IS ACTUALLY PROTECTED
------------------------------------

Under Feist v. Rural Telephone (US 1991) facts are not copyrightable, and a
thin factual compilation attracts thin protection. Separately, the US
Copyright Office holds that text with no human author is not copyrightable,
and the per-lead summaries in the source table were machine-drafted.

So in the United States this license is closer to a stated norm and a citation
request than to an enforceable property claim over every cell. That is
understood and it is fine. The labor worth crediting is the selection, the
checking, and the human sign-off on every published row, and the norm is what
carries that credit. In the EU the sui generis database right does more work,
which is the other reason for 4.0.
